**assistant**:
> The most likely buffer overrun is here:
> 
> ```cpp
> nc_get_var_double(ncid, latVar, latValues.data());
> ```
> 
> You assume each coordinate variable is 1-D with exactly `rows` / `cols` entries. If the file has 2-D curvilinear `lat`/`lon` variables, `nc_get_var_double` writes `rows * cols` values into a vector of only `rows` (or `cols`) doubles, corrupting memory and producing a later segfault.
> 
> Validate coordinate-variable dimensions before reading them:
> 
> ```cpp
> auto readCoordinate = [&](int coordVar, int expectedDim, size_t length,
>                           const char* label) {
>   nc_type coordType;
>   int coordNdims, coordDimids[NC_MAX_VAR_DIMS], coordNatts;
> 
>   int s = nc_inq_var(ncid, coordVar, nullptr, &coordType, &coordNdims,
>                      coordDimids, &coordNatts);
>   if (s != NC_NOERR || coordNdims != 1 || coordDimids[0] != expectedDim)
>     throw runtime_error(string("Expected a 1-D ") + label +
>                         " coordinate variable matching its data dimension.");
> 
>   vector<double> result(length);
>   s = nc_get_var_double(ncid, coordVar, result.data());
>   if (s != NC_NOERR)
>     throw runtime_error(string("Could not read ") + label +
>                         " coordinates: " + nc_strerror(s));
>   return result;
> };
> 
> vector<double> latValues = readCoordinate(latVar, dimids[latDim], rows, "latitude");
> vector<double> lonValues = readCoordinate(lonVar, dimids[lonDim], cols, "longitude");
> ```
> 
> Also check every NetCDF call currently ignored, especially:
> 
> ```cpp
> status = nc_inq_dimname(ncid, dimids[latDim], latName);
> if (status != NC_NOERR) ...
> 
> status = nc_inq_dimname(ncid, dimids[lonDim], lonName);
> if (status != NC_NOERR) ...
> ```
> 
> To confirm, inspect the file:
> 
> ```bash
> ncdump -h your-file.nc
> ```
> 
> You want something like:
> 
> ```text
> double latitude(latitude) ;
> double longitude(longitude) ;
> float variable(latitude, longitude) ;
> ```
> 
> If instead it has:
> 
> ```text
> double lat(y, x) ;
> double lon(y, x) ;
> ```
> 
> it is a curvilinear grid and cannot be represented by your `DividedRange`/rectangular-coordinate model without a different loader or regridding.
> 
> Compile once with AddressSanitizer for an exact offending line:
> 
> ```bash
> clang++ -fsanitize=address -g ...
> ```
> 
> That should turn the opaque segfault into a clear heap-buffer-overflow report.
