> That loop is safe for `buffer` and `sourceColumn` provided their construction succeeded. The likely invalid access is the destination:
> 
> ```cpp
> result->values.get(rr, cc)
> ```
> 
> Most likely `rangeFromCenters()` constructs a `DividedRange` with **one fewer divisions than coordinate centers**. If `DividedRange::withEnds(start, end, width, ...)` interprets `start` and `end` as cell boundaries, then $N$ center coordinates span only $N-1$ widths.
> 
> For center coordinates, construct boundary ends instead:
> 
> ```cpp
> return DividedRange::withEnds(
>     centers.front() - width / 2.0,
>     centers.back()  + width / 2.0,
>     width,
>     indicator);
> ```
> 
> That makes a coordinate vector such as:
> 
> ```text
> 0.5, 1.5, 2.5     // 3 centers
> ```
> 
> map to bounds `[0, 3]` with width `1`, i.e. 3 cells—not `[0.5, 2.5]`, which contains only 2 intervals.
> 
> Immediately before the loop, verify the allocation dimensions against the NetCDF dimensions, using whatever dimension API `values` exposes:
> 
> ```cpp
> assert(result->values.rows() == rows);
> assert(result->values.cols() == cols);
> assert(buffer.size() == rows * cols);
> assert(sourceColumn.size() == cols);
> ```
> 
> Also, temporarily split the indexing to identify precisely which side fails:
> 
> ```cpp
> for (size_t rr = 0; rr < rows; ++rr) {
>   for (size_t cc = 0; cc < cols; ++cc) {
>     size_t source = sourceColumn.at(cc);
>     double value = buffer.at(rr * cols + source);
>     result->values.get(rr, cc) = value;
>   }
> }
> ```
> 
> Use `size_t`, not `unsigned`, since NetCDF dimensions and vector indices are `size_t`.
> 
> If the asserts show a mismatch, the fix belongs in `rangeFromCenters` / `DividedRange` construction, not in the copy loop.
