**assistant**:
> Yes—this is very likely floating-point truncation in `DividedRange::count()`:
> 
> ```cpp
> return (int) ((max - min) / widths);
> ```
> 
> For your longitude grid, `withEnds` produces approximately:
> 
> ```text
> min = -0.05
> max = 359.95
> width = 0.1
> ```
> 
> Mathematically, `(max - min) / width == 3600`, but in binary floating point it may be `3599.9999999999995`. Casting to `int` truncates it to `3599`.
> 
> Then `MatrixGeographicMap` allocates 3599 columns, while the NetCDF loop writes 3600 columns, causing the segfault.
> 
> Replace `count()` with rounding rather than truncation:
> 
> ```cpp
> unsigned count() const {
>   double rawCount = (max - min) / widths;
>   long long roundedCount = std::llround(rawCount);
> 
>   if (roundedCount < 0 ||
>       std::abs(rawCount - roundedCount) > 1e-8 * std::max(1.0, std::abs(rawCount)))
>     throw runtime_error("DividedRange does not contain an integral number of cells.");
> 
>   return static_cast<unsigned>(roundedCount);
> }
> ```
> 
> You will need:
> 
> ```cpp
> #include <algorithm>
> #include <stdexcept>
> ```
> 
> Optionally, make the relevant variables `size_t` throughout the NetCDF loader:
> 
> ```cpp
> for (size_t rr = 0; rr < rows; ++rr)
>   for (size_t cc = 0; cc < cols; ++cc)
>     result->values.get(rr, cc) =
>       buffer[rr * cols + sourceColumn[cc]];
> ```
> 
> A quick confirmation before constructing `result`:
> 
> ```cpp
> std::cerr << "NetCDF: " << rows << " x " << cols << "\n";
> std::cerr << "Ranges: " << latitudes.count()
>           << " x " << longitudes.count() << "\n";
> ```
> 
> I would also remove the “add an extra point” logic in `withEnds`; it attempts to patch this same numerical issue but does not reliably do so. Rounding in `count()` is the correct place to resolve a near-integer cell count.
